// The wired-in `prelude` module. Every operator desugars to one of these functions, and the module
// is default-imported into every user module (so `prelude.add` and stdlib submodules resolve).
//
// Generic bounds give operators precise typing: an arithmetic operator over `T extends number`
// preserves `integer` when both operands are integers, and widens to `number` otherwise.
//
// PRIMARY-ARGUMENT NAMING — the one rule, so a caller never memorizes it per module: the argument an
// agent operates ON is named `value` when it is a SCALAR the agent reads as a whole (a string, a
// number, a JSON value being rendered — `string.trim(value = ...)`, `math.floor(value = ...)`,
// `json.stringify(value = ...)`), and `target` when it is a STRUCTURE the agent reaches INTO (an
// array, a record, a store subtree, a JSON tree being navigated — `array.length(target = ...)`,
// `record.get(target = ..., key = ...)`, `json.field(target = ..., key = ...)`). New stdlib and
// package agents follow the same split.

// Arithmetic -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@"Add two numbers; preserves integer when both operands are integers."
primitive agent add[T extends number](left: T, right: T) -> T
@"Subtract two numbers; preserves integer when both operands are integers."
primitive agent subtract[T extends number](left: T, right: T) -> T
@"Multiply two numbers; preserves integer when both operands are integers."
primitive agent multiply[T extends number](left: T, right: T) -> T
@"Floor-modulo two numbers; preserves integer when both operands are integers. A zero divisor
panics (guard with a comparison first)."
primitive agent modulo[T extends number](@"The dividend." left: T, @"The divisor." right: T) -> T
@"Divide two numbers; always yields a number. A zero divisor panics (guard with a comparison
first) — division never yields Infinity / NaN."
primitive agent divide(@"The dividend." left: number, @"The divisor." right: number) -> number
@"Negate a number; preserves integer."
primitive agent negate[T extends number](value: T) -> T

// Comparison -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@"Whether two values are equal."
primitive agent equal[T](left: T, right: T) -> boolean
@"Whether two values are not equal."
primitive agent not_equal[T](left: T, right: T) -> boolean
// The comparison order is total and deterministic: numbers compare numerically, strings compare by
// Unicode code point (no locale — the string module's declared unit), and when a union instantiation
// mixes the kinds, every number orders before every string. `array.sort` / `array.sort_entries` sort
// by this same order.

@"Whether the left value is less than the right (numbers numerically, strings by Unicode code point;
in a mixed union, numbers order before strings)."
primitive agent less_than[T extends number | string](left: T, right: T) -> boolean
@"Whether the left value is less than or equal to the right (the order of `less_than`)."
primitive agent less_or_equal[T extends number | string](left: T, right: T) -> boolean
@"Whether the left value is greater than the right (the order of `less_than`)."
primitive agent greater_than[T extends number | string](left: T, right: T) -> boolean
@"Whether the left value is greater than or equal to the right (the order of `less_than`)."
primitive agent greater_or_equal[T extends number | string](left: T, right: T) -> boolean

// Logical ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@"Logical conjunction."
primitive agent and(left: boolean, right: boolean) -> boolean
@"Logical disjunction."
primitive agent or(left: boolean, right: boolean) -> boolean
@"Logical negation."
primitive agent not(value: boolean) -> boolean

// String -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

@"Concatenate two strings."
primitive agent concat(left: string, right: string) -> string

// Errors -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The two error channels:
//
//   - `throw[T]` (below) is the error channel for programs: anticipated, domain-specific failures a
//     caller may recover from. The payload type is an error `data` declared next to the operation
//     that fails (`json.parse_error`, `http.fetch_error`, ...). Raises in one scope join to the
//     payload union (`throw[a] | throw[b]` is `throw[a | b]`), and a handler discharges exactly the
//     payload its parameter annotation names — handle the whole union, or rethrow the rest.
//
//   - `panic` is the runtime's own failure signal and has no declaration here on purpose: a program
//     can never RAISE one, and can never name it in an effect row. The runtime panics on invariant
//     violations (a non-exhaustive match, a zero divisor, an FFI infrastructure error, an engine
//     backstop), and an unhandled panic fails the run. Repairing a broken invariant is not a
//     program-level concern; re-running is.
//
//     A panic CAN be OBSERVED, though, by the one clause that names it: the ambient
//     `request panic(msg) { ... }`, addable to any `use handler`. Because `panic` is undeclared the
//     checker recognizes that bare clause structurally, types it from a wired-in signature —
//     `panic(msg: string) -> never`, so the parameter must be spelled `msg` and only an explicit
//     `break` recovers — and keeps it OUT of the continuation's effect row. That last part is what
//     makes it addable anywhere: no signature gains a `panic`, and a caller pays nothing for a
//     handler that catches one. Use it where a crash must be CONTAINED rather than repaired: an AI
//     tool loop turns a crashed tool into a message the model reads and works around (the `ai`
//     package's tool desk does exactly this), a supervisor turns one into a replay signal. The
//     work that panicked is over either way — containing the panic keeps the rest of the run alive,
//     it does not undo the invariant that broke.

@"Signal a typed error the caller may recover from. The payload is a domain-specific error `data`
(e.g. `json.parse_error`); a handler at that payload type catches it — `use handler { request
throw(error: my_error) -> never { break fallback } }` — and an unhandled throw fails the run with
the payload. `-> never` means a throw cannot be resumed: a handler recovers by `break`ing out of
the handle (or rethrows)."
request throw[T](@"The error payload; a handler naming this payload type catches it." error: T) -> never

@"Run @task@ and fold its `throw` into the RESULT: the answer is @task@'s value, or — when it threw —
the thrown payload itself, as a plain `T | Err` union to `match` on. This is the ONE general way a
program turns the error CHANNEL into an error VALUE, and it exists because the hand-written form is
four lines of ceremony whose only variation is a type name: install a handler, `break` the payload out
of the handle, and re-spell the payload type twice. Written `catch(task = agent () -> ... { ... })`,
the whole thing is one expression.

WHAT IT REPLACES, and why they are gone: a total twin of a throwing reader (`json.try_validate` beside
`json.validate`), a bulk pre-check that folds every failure to a boolean (`credentials.check`), a
`_as`-suffixed variant that validates a request's answer — each was the same fold, specialised once per
call site. One general form means the composition is written where it is needed, in the caller's own
words, instead of being anticipated per operation.

IT CATCHES THE WHOLE THROW ROW, not a chosen part of it: the row is `E lacks prelude.throw`, so
whatever @task@ can throw comes back as @Err@ and nothing throwing survives into @catch@'s own row.
Partial capture — catch these payloads, let the rest keep flying — is NOT expressible here and stays a
hand-written handler with a rethrow arm, because the union would have to be split against a rigid type
variable. That is the deliberate line: `catch` is for the boundary where a failure BECOMES data, a
handler is for the boundary where a failure is REROUTED.

TWO THINGS TO KNOW. A @task@ that throws NOTHING leaves @Err@ with no lower bound and fails to infer
(K3016) — write `catch[T, never]` if you really mean it, though catching a task that cannot throw says
nothing worth saying. And the answer is a bare union, so an instantiation where @T@ and @Err@ OVERLAP
cannot tell the two apart afterwards — the ordinary shapes do not (a value and a `data` error are
structurally disjoint), but a `catch[unknown, ...]` deliberately blurs them."
agent catch[T, Err, effect E lacks prelude.throw](
  @"The work to run; its throws are folded into the result. Written inline as an anonymous nullary agent (`agent () -> T with ... { ... }`) — the `(value: null)` here is the accepting bound, not a shape to imitate." task: agent (value: null) -> T with E | prelude.throw[Err],
) -> T | Err with E {
  // The whole mechanism: one handler that `break`s the payload out of the handle, so a throw leaves as
  // the handle's value. Nothing resumes — `throw` is `-> never` — so there is no other way out.
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: Err) -> never {
      break error
    }
  }
  task(value = null)
}

